<section xml:id="sec-mod8-worked-examples">
  <title>Module 8: Worked Examples</title>
  <p>Annotated Proof Walkthroughs — Estimated time: 40 minutes</p>
    <p>
      Read each example slowly. The goal is to internalize the proof
      template, not just to see the answer. Example 1 is the archetype
      of a direct Darboux argument; Example 2 shows the proof that
      continuous implies integrable; Example 3 proves FTC Part 1 in full;
      Example 4 shows FTC Part 2 in a computation.
    </p>
  <!-- ============================================================
       EXAMPLE 1: f(x)=x IS RIEMANN INTEGRABLE
  ============================================================ -->

  <exercise xml:id="ex-mod8-we-1">
    <title>Example 1: <m>f(x) = x</m> is Riemann Integrable on <m>[0,1]</m> and <m>\int = 1/2</m></title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Prove from the Darboux definition that <m>f(x) = x</m> is
        Riemann integrable on <m>[0,1]</m> and
        <m>\int_0^1 x\,dx = 1/2</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        <alert>Strategy.</alert>
      </p>
      <p>
        Use uniform partitions <m>P_n</m> with <m>n</m> equal
        subintervals, compute upper and lower sums explicitly, show their
        difference tends to zero, and use the integrability criterion.
        The common limit of <m>U(f,P_n)</m> and <m>L(f,P_n)</m> will
        identify the integral.
      </p>
      <p>
        <alert>Proof.</alert>
      </p>
      <p>
        Let <m>P_n</m> be the uniform partition with points
        <m>x_k = k/n</m> for <m>k = 0, 1, \ldots, n</m>, so each
        subinterval has width <m>\Delta x = 1/n</m>. Since <m>f(x) = x</m>
        is increasing, on the <m>k</m>th subinterval
        <m>[(k-1)/n, k/n]</m>:
        <md>
          M_k = \sup f = \frac{k}{n}, \qquad
          m_k = \inf f = \frac{k-1}{n}.
        </md>
        The upper and lower sums are:
        <md>
          <mrow>
            U(f,P_n)
            \amp= \sum_{k=1}^{n} \frac{k}{n} \cdot \frac{1}{n}
             = \frac{1}{n^2} \sum_{k=1}^{n} k
             = \frac{1}{n^2} \cdot \frac{n(n+1)}{2}
             = \frac{n+1}{2n},
          </mrow>
          <mrow>
            L(f,P_n)
            \amp= \sum_{k=1}^{n} \frac{k-1}{n} \cdot \frac{1}{n}
             = \frac{1}{n^2} \sum_{k=1}^{n} (k-1)
             = \frac{1}{n^2} \cdot \frac{n(n-1)}{2}
             = \frac{n-1}{2n}.
          </mrow>
        </md>
        Therefore
        <md>
          U(f,P_n) - L(f,P_n) = \frac{n+1}{2n} - \frac{n-1}{2n}
          = \frac{2}{2n} = \frac{1}{n} \to 0.
        </md>
        By the integrability criterion, <m>f</m> is Riemann integrable
        on <m>[0,1]</m>. Moreover, since the upper and lower integrals
        are equal (both squeezed between <m>U</m> and <m>L</m>), and
        <md>
          \lim_{n \to \infty} U(f,P_n) = \lim_{n \to \infty} \frac{n+1}{2n} = \frac{1}{2},
          \qquad
          \lim_{n \to \infty} L(f,P_n) = \lim_{n \to \infty} \frac{n-1}{2n} = \frac{1}{2},
        </md>
        the common value of the upper and lower integrals is
        <m>1/2</m>. Hence <m>\int_0^1 x\,dx = 1/2</m>. <m>\square</m>
      </p>
      <p>
        <alert>What to notice.</alert>
        This is the simplest possible integrability proof from the
        definition: the function is increasing, so <m>M_k</m> and
        <m>m_k</m> are just the values at the right and left endpoints.
        The difference <m>U - L = 1/n</m> has a beautifully transparent
        form. In Worked Example 2, the same strategy &#x2014; bound
        <m>M_i - m_i</m> on each subinterval &#x2014; is applied to
        continuous functions, where uniform continuity does the
        bounding.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>

  <!-- ============================================================
       EXAMPLE 2: CONTINUOUS IMPLIES INTEGRABLE
  ============================================================ -->

  <exercise xml:id="ex-mod8-we-2">
    <title>Example 2: Continuous Functions are Riemann Integrable</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Prove that if <m>f</m> is continuous on <m>[a,b]</m>, then
        <m>f</m> is Riemann integrable on <m>[a,b]</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        <alert>Strategy.</alert>
      </p>
      <p>
        Use the Heine<ndash/>Cantor theorem (uniform continuity of
        continuous functions on closed bounded intervals, stated in
        <xref ref="ch-mod6" text="custom">Module 6</xref>; see Zorn or
        MAT 5610 for a proof) to bound the oscillation <m>M_i - m_i</m> on each subinterval,
        then sum up and apply the integrability criterion.
      </p>
      <p>
        <alert>Proof.</alert>
      </p>
      <p>
        Since <m>f</m> is continuous on the closed bounded interval
        <m>[a,b]</m>, the Heine<ndash/>Cantor theorem
        (<xref ref="ex-mod6-sg-34-3"/>, from Zorn &#xa7;3.4) gives that
        <m>f</m> is uniformly continuous on <m>[a,b]</m>. Let
        <m>\varepsilon \gt 0</m> be given. By uniform continuity,
        there exists <m>\delta \gt 0</m> such that for all
        <m>x, y \in [a,b]</m> with <m>|x - y| \lt \delta</m>,
        <md>|f(x) - f(y)| \lt \frac{\varepsilon}{2(b - a)}.</md>
        Choose any partition <m>P = \{x_0, x_1, \ldots, x_n\}</m> of
        <m>[a,b]</m> with <m>\|P\| \lt \delta</m> (such a partition
        exists; take, for instance, a uniform partition with
        <m>n \gt (b-a)/\delta</m>).
      </p>
      <p>
        On each subinterval <m>[x_{i-1}, x_i]</m>, any two points
        <m>x, y</m> satisfy <m>|x - y| \leq \|P\| \lt \delta</m>,
        so <m>|f(x) - f(y)| \lt \varepsilon/(2(b-a))</m>. Taking the
        supremum over <m>x</m> and infimum over <m>y</m> (or vice
        versa) gives <m>M_i - m_i \leq \varepsilon/(2(b-a))</m>.
        Therefore:
        <md>
          U(f,P) - L(f,P)
          = \sum_{i=1}^{n} (M_i - m_i)\,\Delta x_i
          \leq \frac{\varepsilon}{2(b-a)} \sum_{i=1}^{n} \Delta x_i
          = \frac{\varepsilon}{2(b-a)} \cdot (b-a)
          = \frac{\varepsilon}{2}
          \lt \varepsilon.
        </md>
        Since <m>\varepsilon \gt 0</m> was arbitrary and we exhibited
        a partition <m>P</m> with <m>U(f,P) - L(f,P) \lt \varepsilon</m>,
        the integrability criterion is satisfied. Hence <m>f</m> is
        Riemann integrable on <m>[a,b]</m>. <m>\square</m>
      </p>
      <p>
        <alert>What to notice.</alert>
        Uniform continuity is indispensable to this argument: the same
        <m>\delta</m> must work at every point of <m>[a,b]</m>
        simultaneously, which is exactly the guarantee uniform
        continuity provides. Continuity at each point separately gives
        a <m>\delta</m> that depends on the point; those values could
        tend to zero, and then no single mesh would work. (This is a
        limitation of the method, not of continuous functions: on
        <m>[a,b]</m>, the Heine<ndash/>Cantor theorem guarantees the
        single <m>\delta</m>.) The factor <m>2</m> in
        <m>\varepsilon/(2(b-a))</m> is there because taking a supremum
        can turn the strict inequality <m>\lt</m> into <m>\leq</m>;
        aiming for <m>\varepsilon/2</m> keeps the final inequality
        strict. The proof template is:
        (1) invoke Heine<ndash/>Cantor; (2) choose a partition with mesh
        smaller than <m>\delta</m>; (3) bound <m>M_i - m_i</m>;
        (4) sum up; (5) invoke the criterion.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>

  <!-- ============================================================
       EXAMPLE 3: FTC PART 1
  ============================================================ -->

  <exercise xml:id="ex-mod8-we-3">
    <title>Example 3: FTC Part 1</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Prove FTC Part 1: if <m>f</m> is continuous on <m>[a,b]</m>
        and <m>F(x) = \int_a^x f(t)\,dt</m> for <m>x \in [a,b]</m>,
        then <m>F</m> is differentiable on <m>(a,b)</m> and
        <m>F'(x) = f(x)</m> for every <m>x \in (a,b)</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        <alert>Strategy.</alert>
      </p>
      <p>
        Compute <m>(F(x+h) - F(x))/h</m> using the additivity of
        the integral, and show that it approaches <m>f(x)</m> as
        <m>h \to 0</m>. The key is that <m>f(x)</m> can be written as
        the average value of the constant <m>f(x)</m> over
        <m>[x, x+h]</m>, so the difference quotient minus <m>f(x)</m>
        is controlled by how close <m>f(t)</m> is to <m>f(x)</m>
        for <m>t</m> near <m>x</m> &#x2014; which is exactly what
        continuity of <m>f</m> at <m>x</m> gives.
      </p>
      <p>
        <alert>Proof.</alert>
      </p>
      <p>
        Fix <m>x \in (a,b)</m> and consider <m>h \neq 0</m> with
        <m>x + h \in [a,b]</m>. By additivity of the integral
        and the orientation convention (<xref ref="par-mod8-sg-54-props"
        text="custom">Properties of the Integral Used in the FTC
        Proofs</xref>), for <m>h</m> of either sign,
        <md>
          F(x+h) - F(x)
          = \int_a^{x+h} f(t)\,dt - \int_a^x f(t)\,dt
          = \int_x^{x+h} f(t)\,dt.
        </md>
        Dividing by <m>h</m>:
        <md>
          \frac{F(x+h) - F(x)}{h} = \frac{1}{h}\int_x^{x+h} f(t)\,dt.
        </md>
        Since <m>f(x)</m> is constant with respect to <m>t</m>, the
        integral of a constant gives
        <m>\int_x^{x+h} f(x)\,dt = h f(x)</m>. For <m>h \gt 0</m> this
        is the integral of a constant over <m>[x, x+h]</m>; for
        <m>h \lt 0</m>, the orientation convention gives
        <md>
          \int_x^{x+h} f(x)\,dt = -\int_{x+h}^x f(x)\,dt
          = -f(x)\bigl(x - (x+h)\bigr) = h f(x).
        </md>
        So
        <m>f(x) = \dfrac{1}{h}\int_x^{x+h} f(x)\,dt</m> and, by
        linearity (which also holds for <m>h \lt 0</m>: by the orientation
        convention, negate both sides of linearity on <m>[x+h, x]</m>),
        <md>
          \frac{F(x+h) - F(x)}{h} - f(x)
          = \frac{1}{h}\int_x^{x+h} [f(t) - f(x)]\,dt.
        </md>
        Let <m>\varepsilon \gt 0</m>. Since <m>f</m> is continuous at
        <m>x</m>, there exists <m>\delta \gt 0</m> such that
        <m>|t - x| \lt \delta \Rightarrow |f(t) - f(x)| \lt
        \varepsilon</m>. Shrinking <m>\delta</m> if necessary, we may
        assume <m>\delta \leq \min(x - a, b - x)</m>, so that
        <m>x + h \in [a,b]</m> whenever <m>|h| \lt \delta</m>. Let
        <m>0 \lt |h| \lt \delta</m>, and let
        <m>I_h</m> be the closed interval with endpoints <m>x</m> and
        <m>x + h</m>, which has length <m>|h|</m>. For <m>t \in I_h</m>
        we have <m>|t - x| \leq |h| \lt \delta</m>, so
        <m>-\varepsilon \lt f(t) - f(x) \lt \varepsilon</m>. The
        function <m>f(t) - f(x)</m> is continuous, hence integrable, on
        <m>I_h</m> (<xref ref="ex-mod8-we-2" text="custom">Worked
        Example 2</xref>), so monotonicity and the integral of a constant
        give
        <md>
          -\varepsilon |h| \leq \int_{I_h} [f(t) - f(x)]\,dt \leq \varepsilon |h|,
        </md>
        where <m>\int_{I_h}</m> is the integral from the left endpoint
        of <m>I_h</m> to the right one. The integral
        <m>\int_x^{x+h}</m> equals <m>\int_{I_h}</m> if <m>h \gt 0</m>
        and <m>-\int_{I_h}</m> if <m>h \lt 0</m>, so in both cases
        <m>\left|\int_x^{x+h}[f(t) - f(x)]\,dt\right| \leq \varepsilon|h|</m>.
        Therefore:
        <md>
          \left|\frac{F(x+h) - F(x)}{h} - f(x)\right|
          = \frac{1}{|h|}\left|\int_x^{x+h}[f(t) - f(x)]\,dt\right|
          \leq \frac{1}{|h|} \cdot \varepsilon |h|
          = \varepsilon.
        </md>
        Since <m>\varepsilon \gt 0</m> was arbitrary, the limit of
        the difference quotient is <m>f(x)</m>, i.e.,
        <m>F'(x) = f(x)</m>. <m>\square</m>
      </p>
      <p>
        <alert>What to notice.</alert>
        The proof has two moving parts: the additivity identity
        <m>\int_a^{x+h} - \int_a^x = \int_x^{x+h}</m>, and the
        continuity estimate that makes <m>|f(t) - f(x)|</m> small
        when <m>|t - x|</m> is small. The bound
        <m>|(1/h)\int_x^{x+h} g\,dt| \leq \sup|g|</m> (averaging
        an estimate over a small interval preserves the estimate) is
        the analytic core of the argument.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>

  <paragraphs xml:id="par-mod8-we-base-point">
    <title>FTC Part 1 with Any Base Point</title>
    <p>
      <xref ref="ex-mod8-we-3" text="custom">Worked Example 3</xref>
      measures the integral from the left endpoint <m>a</m>. With the
      orientation convention, the base point can be any point of the
      interval. Suppose <m>f</m> is continuous on an interval
      <m>J</m>, <m>p \in J</m>, and <m>G(x) = \int_p^x f(t)\,dt</m> for
      <m>x \in J</m>. If <m>x</m> is an interior point of <m>J</m>,
      choose <m>r, s \in J</m> with <m>r \leq p \leq s</m> and
      <m>r \lt x \lt s</m>. Then
      <m>G(x) = \int_r^x f(t)\,dt - \int_r^p f(t)\,dt</m>, and the
      second term does not depend on <m>x</m>, so Worked Example 3 on
      <m>[r,s]</m> gives <m>G'(x) = f(x)</m>. This is the form of
      FTC Part 1 to use with the chain rule when the upper limit can
      fall below the base point.
    </p>
  </paragraphs>

  <!-- ============================================================
       EXAMPLE 4: COMPUTATION USING FTC
  ============================================================ -->

  <exercise xml:id="ex-mod8-we-4">
    <title>Example 4: A Computation Using FTC</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Compute <m>\int_0^{\pi} \sin(x)\,dx</m> using FTC Part 2.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        <alert>Strategy.</alert>
      </p>
      <p>
        Find an antiderivative <m>F</m> with <m>F'(x) = \sin(x)</m>,
        verify that <m>f(x) = \sin(x)</m> is continuous on
        <m>[0, \pi]</m>, and apply FTC Part 2.
      </p>
      <p>
        <alert>Proof.</alert>
      </p>
      <p>
        Take <m>F(x) = -\cos(x)</m>. We verify: <m>F'(x) = \sin(x)</m>
        (by the formula <m>\cos'(x) = -\sin(x)</m> from calculus;
        <xref ref="ch-mod7" text="custom">Module 7</xref> uses the
        derivatives of <m>\sin</m> and <m>\cos</m> but does not prove
        them). The function <m>f(x) = \sin(x)</m> is continuous
        on <m>[0, \pi]</m> (it is continuous everywhere). Since
        <m>F'(x) = \sin(x) = f(x)</m> on <m>[0, \pi]</m>, FTC Part 2
        applies:
        <md>
          \int_0^{\pi} \sin(x)\,dx
          = F(\pi) - F(0)
          = -\cos(\pi) - (-\cos(0))
          = -(-1) - (-1)
          = 1 + 1
          = 2.
        </md>
        <m>\square</m>
      </p>
      <p>
        <alert>What to notice.</alert>
        The proof template for applying FTC Part 2 has three steps:
        (1) exhibit an antiderivative <m>F</m> and verify
        <m>F' = f</m>; (2) check that <m>f</m> is continuous on
        <m>[a,b]</m> (the hypothesis of FTC Part 2); (3) compute
        <m>F(b) - F(a)</m>. Step (2) is often omitted in calculus
        courses, but continuity of <m>f</m> is a hypothesis of FTC
        Part 2 as stated and proved in this module, so it must be
        checked before that theorem applies. (Continuity is sufficient
        for the conclusion, not necessary; the version proved here
        simply assumes it.) In this
        example, <m>\sin</m> is continuous everywhere, so there is
        nothing to check, but for a piecewise-defined function the
        continuity condition would require verification.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>

</section>
